          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:80c3d7c29cb7da5276985d182b113d2f84c1f74be6d33c302691fd499caccc4c:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://scripts/cd/publish-apk.mjs#L1-L67",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:149d34e503ab934ccfdf745ff8c6564a212e7da1871123c3d60a0ada3ba4781c:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_abd4b794891944c1a804f31ccade26e1",
      "statement": "Before rebuilding the APK, check whether the installed APK is already native-compatible with HEAD by diffing package.json, android/ and app.json against the APK's build commit: an empty diff means a pure JS/Metro change and no rebuild is needed. Conversely, Maestro launches the installed APK via am start and never rebuilds it, so after any native change the old native binary runs against the new JS bundle and crashes at startup; expo prebuild --clean neither builds nor installs.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/runbooks/android-emulator.md#L219-L254",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:7df0e5fc69c0cd8073306dcd04ac41507226a5217dfaa9d267be98e94c0a43de:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_473a1702f0f54c0b831f3fa30ab69b61",
      "statement": "Windows hits a hard CMAKE_OBJECT_PATH_MAX (250 character) wall when building RN >=0.85 native modules, because CMake replicates the full absolute source path under the object directory and this repo's path plus the deep pnpm layout overflows it (worst measured 381 characters); Windows LongPathsEnabled does not help because the cap is internal to CMake, so scripts/build-apk-short-path.ps1 automates a short-root junction plus node-linker=hoisted build and always reverts it, since hoisted breaks Metro/jest module resolution.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://docs/runbooks/android-emulator.md#L256-L286",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:a5d0c22a2316a9dbea265ad822285d0ca961f311c358d4a3223fc95767ebe856:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://scripts/build-apk-short-path.ps1#L1-L29",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:dd225e9113ab4f5ac3df622fe1f467ec42ca1af639aef6825f461809027debc1:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://scripts/build-apk-short-path.ps1#L84-L102",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:d1974d593c09468c60986bafa71a547a78c4ee2ae424d35cad8d261f034cceac:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_83a5a509ae4443738d53b85e627afd18",
      "statement": "CI is the recommended APK build path: both Forgejo builds use APK_VARIANT=release so there is no CI debug/Metro-attached APK, and app-e2e's disk-free step is load-bearing because it reclaims space by pruning the rootless Docker daemon rather than by deleting files. It has two halves — `docker image prune -af` and (added 2026-10-03) `docker builder prune -f --reserved-space 50GB` — because image prune never touches the build cache, which had reached 375 GB (355 GB reclaimable) of a shared 914 GB disk; `--reserved-space` retains the most recent 50 GB so warm layers still hit. The `|| true` on both lines is deliberate but not a licence to be wrong: the flag was checked against the docker 27.5.1 CLI these jobs install so a bad flag cannot become a silent no-op. Without the reclamation the native build exhausts runner disk and dies mid-compile.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://.forgejo/workflows/app-ci.yml#L1186-L1205",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:403312d527270c087baf3740fbea9eaecadbf062a593e02bc764053d552c143c:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://.forgejo/workflows/app-ci.yml#L465-L479",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:30b057aa24e9a79869ec0b820ce66b0e65f7fc73d9e65f1cd95e55d4d3be2f6e:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://.forgejo/workflows/app-ci.yml#L481-L492",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:45a01404d54d2695f18f17540731cda1ee7b966c957b43cd1de4f2ba41497d8f:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_99b6c2ad818342309ea8dd88c10abc76",
      "statement": "The retained Docker Desktop / DinD dev container can run the Android emulator but cannot build the APK there: the Android SDK and an android-34 google_apis x86_64 system image plus the Gradle wrapper distribution are baked into the toolchain image at build time (before the egress firewall), and the privileged container exposes the host /dev/kvm, but Gradle still resolves the Android/Maven plugin graph from the open internet, which the default-deny firewall blocks. The primary Docker Sandbox microVM path cannot run the emulator at all because it offers no nested virtualization, which is the sole reason the Docker Desktop path is retained.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://.devcontainer/toolchain.Dockerfile#L170-L241",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:e7e32e8bb37e8002b45a3cb89779d0798795d1d4826e0fa2b8bdf04a2a36d39b:"
        },
